<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">A shorter measuring ruler follows more of the bends.</p>
<svg viewBox="0 0 470 220" class="w-full" role="img" aria-labelledby="coast-title coast-desc">
<title id="coast-title">Coarse and fine approximations of one coastline</title><desc id="coast-desc">A coarse ruler replaces the bends with a few straight segments, while a smaller ruler traces more of the details, producing a longer total measurement.</desc>
<path d="M20 145 L80 60 L140 125 L215 48 L280 125 L355 70 L450 145" fill="none" stroke="#d7a13d" stroke-width="11" stroke-linejoin="round" opacity=".45"/>
<path d="M20 145 L80 60 L140 125 L215 48 L280 125 L355 70 L450 145" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="8 7"/>
<path d="M20 145 L40 115 L51 130 L80 60 L90 86 L105 79 L117 110 L140 125 L160 90 L178 105 L197 70 L215 48 L229 84 L244 65 L257 106 L280 125 L297 92 L319 110 L335 80 L355 70 L377 106 L398 91 L421 131 L450 145" fill="none" stroke="#bd4b37" stroke-width="3" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
<g fill="currentColor" font-size="16"><text x="25" y="28">Dashed: big steps</text><text x="285" y="28">Red: smaller steps</text><text x="25" y="197">Same endpoints. Different total lengths.</text></g>
</svg>
<div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-base"><thead><tr class="border-b border-ink"><th class="p-2 text-left">Example ruler length</th><th class="p-2 text-right">Quoted measured coast</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-b border-ink"><td class="p-2">100 km</td><td class="p-2 text-right">about 2,800 km</td></tr><tr><td class="p-2">50 km</td><td class="p-2 text-right">about 3,400 km</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">The red zigzag is a schematic, not a map of Britain. Smaller rulers pick up bends missed by bigger ones. A mathematical coast with detail at every scale may never approach a finite length; real shores require a chosen measuring scale.</figcaption>
</figure>